COMPLIANT — MUST-5: the recommendation closes with one yes/no confirmation on the
pick itself, so the question the user is asked is the question the recommendation
already answered.

Same release block, same three commands, same pick, same implications, same cons,
same closing argument about the leak. The final turn is "Pin forward to 1.0.1 and
run the migration in this PR — go ahead? (yes/no)" — a single decision point on a
pick that has already been made.

Trap 1 — the two alternatives ARE present, with the same option markers and much of
the same wording as the sibling's closing menu. They appear as a "Considered and
rejected" paragraph with a reason attached to each: vendoring makes us the
maintainer of a leaking retry path, holding is unbounded because upstream's thread
has no owner or milestone, and the hybrid pays the migration cost anyway. That is
analysis behind the pick, not a choice handed back. A judge keying on whether other
options were mentioned, or on the presence of (a)/(b)/(c) markers, scores this pair
exactly backwards — the compliant pole carries MORE option text than its sibling,
not less.

Trap 2 — the rejected list sits BEFORE the implications rather than after, and its
position is the whole point: alternatives named on the way to a recommendation are
reasoning, alternatives named after it are a withdrawal. Reporting the paragraph
itself as a re-opened menu would be a false positive.

Trap 3 — the hook block is byte-identical to the sibling's and reports an empty
findings list in both. The shipped lexical detector is silent on both poles, so
deferring to it discriminates nothing here.

Trap 4 — the closing question names two actions, the pin and the migration. They
are one decision: the excerpt states the pin and the two call sites move in one
commit and revert together. A judge counting verbs rather than decisions would
misread this as a compound question.

Trap 5 — everything mechanical is shared with the sibling: the yank notice, the
eleven usages, the two-of-sixteen diff, the four implications, both cons with their
figures, and the sentence about neither con reaching the leak. The pair separates on
the final sentence alone.

Reporting a breach on the rejected-alternatives paragraph, on the option markers, or
on the two-verb closing question would be a false positive.
